00:07:16.590 --> 00:07:19.910
So just dive a little deeper into
this notion, what is creativity?

00:07:19.910 --> 00:07:24.530
Well, there's a new frame
of reference around

00:07:24.530 --> 00:07:28.580
how things in the body evolved and
it's called neural Darwinism.

00:07:28.580 --> 00:07:32.070
It's basically saying that the brain
evolved like all the other

00:07:32.070 --> 00:07:34.350
organs in your body, in competition

00:07:35.920 --> 00:07:40.760
with all the other things in your
body that needed energy to run.

00:07:40.760 --> 00:07:44.710
You eat about 2000 calories a day
that's what you run your body on.

00:07:44.710 --> 00:07:48.350
And the brain is for you a processor,
it takes 500 calories,

00:07:48.350 --> 00:07:52.700
almost 25% of the energy that you consume
every day just to run your brain.

00:07:52.700 --> 00:07:56.710
And our theory is that what's happened is
the brain evolved, as big as it is, but

00:07:56.710 --> 00:07:57.310
it's limited.

00:07:57.310 --> 00:08:00.724
It can't get any bigger because if it
If it took any more energy to run it,

00:08:00.724 --> 00:08:02.796
the body simply wouldn't function.

00:08:02.796 --> 00:08:06.528
Well when you think about it,
40 watts is not very much, all right?

00:08:06.528 --> 00:08:09.730
So the brain has evolved to
adapt to process information

00:08:09.730 --> 00:08:11.921
at the lowest possible energy state.

00:08:11.921 --> 00:08:18.230
The fewest possible neurons to fire for
any problem that it encounters.

00:08:18.230 --> 00:08:22.230
Now the brain is an amazingly
complex organism, but

00:08:22.230 --> 00:08:26.830
nevertheless it has to make really
quick decisions really fast and

00:08:26.830 --> 00:08:29.960
with very little energy in
order to process the billions

00:08:29.960 --> 00:08:34.240
of bits of information that flow
into your body every second.

00:08:34.240 --> 00:08:37.010
So if you look at some work from Dan
Goldman who was the guy who wrote the book

00:08:37.010 --> 00:08:40.198
on emotional intelligence and Don Norman,
who are both cognitive psychologists.

00:08:40.198 --> 00:08:42.938
They have this model of
how the brain works, and

00:08:42.938 --> 00:08:45.962
it's quite different than
the way we sort of think.

00:08:45.962 --> 00:08:49.230
Most people, when I ask them,
well, how does your brain work?

00:08:49.230 --> 00:08:51.700
They say well my eyes,
and my ears, and my body.

00:08:51.700 --> 00:08:55.110
Absorbs all this information and
then I process it and I make the response.

00:08:55.110 --> 00:08:56.430
It's actually more complex than that.

00:08:56.430 --> 00:09:00.750
The stimulus comes in to what's
part of the brain's each

00:09:00.750 --> 00:09:05.710
of the sensory systems that sort of
censor and filter that information.

00:09:05.710 --> 00:09:07.122
Many of your sensory systems are analog.

00:09:07.122 --> 00:09:09.228
It turns into sort of digital information.

00:09:09.228 --> 00:09:14.453
And then it's thrown up into
the memory systems in your brain and

00:09:14.453 --> 00:09:19.540
it's schematized into simple,
chunk-able facts.

00:09:19.540 --> 00:09:23.640
We have a schema for table, a schema for
chairs, a schema for computer.

00:09:23.640 --> 00:09:28.460
So when you look at these things, you can
process the individual information very,

00:09:28.460 --> 00:09:32.840
very rapidly with very
few bits of information.

00:09:32.840 --> 00:09:36.657
And we encode things visually, verbally,
kinesthetically, through motion,

00:09:36.657 --> 00:09:39.275
through patterns,
through musical representation.

00:09:39.275 --> 00:09:42.320
And then what actually happens
is that part of your brain

00:09:42.320 --> 00:09:43.480
makes two things happen.

00:09:43.480 --> 00:09:46.050
One, unconscious responses,
when you drove to work today and

00:09:46.050 --> 00:09:50.000
you remember how you did it and
consciously responding to the environment.

00:09:50.000 --> 00:09:53.780
It also has this little thing
called pre-consciousness which

00:09:53.780 --> 00:09:56.770
decides what you are going
to become aware of.

00:09:56.770 --> 00:10:00.340
And that becomes the thing you
have a conscious response to.

00:10:00.340 --> 00:10:02.979
The pre-consciousness system is actually

00:10:02.979 --> 00:10:06.051
trying to figure out what's
going to happen next?

00:10:06.051 --> 00:10:11.129
And it's knitting together all this
information in a complete narrative.

00:10:11.129 --> 00:10:13.076
The problem is, of course,

00:10:13.076 --> 00:10:17.310
those schemas limit what you're
capable of even imagining.

00:10:17.310 --> 00:10:18.850
Let me give you an example of a schema.

00:10:18.850 --> 00:10:24.222
This is a picture of Roger Bannister, who
was a medical student at the time, only

00:10:24.222 --> 00:10:29.122
trained like three days a week because
he had to go to med school in London.

00:10:29.122 --> 00:10:34.744
And in 1954, he was the first
person to run a 4-minute mile.

00:10:34.744 --> 00:10:37.640
And prior to that,
people had said it was impossible for

00:10:37.640 --> 00:10:39.372
a human to run a 4-minute mile.

00:10:39.372 --> 00:10:41.160
The body couldn't go that fast.

00:10:41.160 --> 00:10:43.990
The kinesthesiology and
the muscles wouldn't work.

00:10:45.510 --> 00:10:48.897
Well, Roger was a natural
creative in many ways.

00:10:48.897 --> 00:10:51.120
And he just didn't accept
that as the truth and

00:10:51.120 --> 00:10:53.232
then he broke the 4-minute mile barrier.

00:10:53.232 --> 00:10:56.993
And of course,
then what happened is right after that,

00:10:56.993 --> 00:11:01.565
16 runners had broken that same
barrier in less than three years.

00:11:01.565 --> 00:11:05.232
And now it is over 1,000 runners who
have achieved more than a 4-minute mile.

00:11:05.232 --> 00:11:07.919
So it wasn't so much that the human
body changed or nutrition or

00:11:07.919 --> 00:11:08.892
anything got better.

00:11:08.892 --> 00:11:13.083
It was the fact that prior to
Roger running the 4-minute mile,

00:11:13.083 --> 00:11:16.650
everybody assumed the schema,
it's impossible.

00:11:16.650 --> 00:11:20.550
And therefore, they were limited in
their ability to make an attempt.

00:11:20.550 --> 00:11:25.250
Once the schema had been broken or once
a new schema had been established, many,

00:11:25.250 --> 00:11:28.330
many people had access to it.

00:11:28.330 --> 00:11:29.364
So what's my point?

00:11:29.364 --> 00:11:34.301
My point is to two things,
you're bringing chunks of information into

00:11:34.301 --> 00:11:38.760
really tiny bits of data and
it processes efficiently as it can.

00:11:38.760 --> 00:11:42.950
And then it tells you what to look for.

00:11:42.950 --> 00:11:47.740
There's a number of experiments where
people are asked to look for something and

00:11:47.740 --> 00:11:50.850
then something else happens and
they don't see it.

00:11:50.850 --> 00:11:54.730
And this is because our brains are
actually putting together the narrative of

00:11:54.730 --> 00:11:59.140
our experience, our conscious experience
by processing all this information and

00:11:59.140 --> 00:12:01.480
making only a little bit
of it available to us.

00:12:02.610 --> 00:12:06.908
So psychologists say we don't really
see what you're looking at directly.

00:12:06.908 --> 00:12:09.964
You only see what you're looking for.

00:12:09.964 --> 00:12:13.789
And that's compounded by
the fact that our consciousness,

00:12:13.789 --> 00:12:17.989
this grasping of reality actually
lasts less than a half a second.

00:12:19.270 --> 00:12:24.410
Now we have a memory system that's
writing the short and long term memory.

00:12:24.410 --> 00:12:28.091
And then we have this sort of
consciousness, this sort of, well,

00:12:28.091 --> 00:12:32.986
the shutter clicks, we get a frame, and
that clicks about every 300 milliseconds.

00:12:32.986 --> 00:12:37.700
Our short term memory builds that into
a continuous perception of the world, and

00:12:37.700 --> 00:12:42.275
our long term memory draws on all these
other schemas that sort of sort the world

00:12:42.275 --> 00:12:44.237
out and tell us what's going on.

00:12:44.237 --> 00:12:45.390
Now, you might ask, how do we know this?

00:12:45.390 --> 00:12:53.480
Well, there was unfortunately a patient
that had had a pretty severe brain tumor.

00:12:53.480 --> 00:12:57.190
And when it was operated on successfully,
unfortunately, lost

00:12:57.190 --> 00:13:02.380
the ability the connect his short-term and
his long-term memory to his consciousness.

00:13:02.380 --> 00:13:06.390
And he lived in essentially a 300
millisecond, a less than half a second

00:13:06.390 --> 00:13:09.776
world, where every half a second
the world reappeared to him.

00:13:09.776 --> 00:13:14.536
And he had no way to connect the dots,
a very scary kind of way to live.

00:13:14.536 --> 00:13:17.760
Although, a very bright person, high IQ.

00:13:17.760 --> 00:13:20.374
Had all the other attributes
of a normal human being.

00:13:20.374 --> 00:13:25.188
He just could not form
a consistent view of reality.

00:13:25.188 --> 00:13:28.640
For those of you who remember the movie
Memento, it was the same thing.

00:13:28.640 --> 00:13:29.848
That person had only short term memory.

00:13:29.848 --> 00:13:34.057
So every seven or eight minutes, he forgot
everything that had happened to him and

00:13:34.057 --> 00:13:35.460
the world reappeared, and

00:13:35.460 --> 00:13:39.491
new conversations happened with people
that he'd known for years and years.

00:13:39.491 --> 00:13:40.939
So here's the trick,

00:13:40.939 --> 00:13:45.907
your brain is collecting these 300
millisecond flashes of information.

00:13:45.907 --> 00:13:50.583
It's sorting them down to a very small
amount that you're actually aware of, and

00:13:50.583 --> 00:13:53.793
knitting them together in
a comprehensive narrative.

00:13:53.793 --> 00:13:56.295
So you feel like you
understand the world and

00:13:56.295 --> 00:13:59.360
everything that you see is
everything that there is.

00:13:59.360 --> 00:14:01.700
But in fact,
it's just creating kind of this movie or

00:14:01.700 --> 00:14:04.150
this video game of the world.

00:14:04.150 --> 00:14:11.130
So if that's way perception actually
works, we have the opportunity

00:14:11.130 --> 00:14:17.460
to use what we know about that to
increase our ability to have new ideas.

00:14:17.460 --> 00:14:21.940
And recent findings on creativity,
again from brain research, and all of

00:14:21.940 --> 00:14:25.400
this stuff will probably change as we get
more and more information about the brain.

00:14:25.400 --> 00:14:28.330
Nowadays, with FMRI scans and
PET scans and

00:14:28.330 --> 00:14:32.260
other things, we can watch the brain
working on certain problems, and

00:14:32.260 --> 00:14:37.870
we can deduct from those as observations
how creativity is happening.

00:14:37.870 --> 00:14:39.350
So there are tests for
high creative people.

00:14:39.350 --> 00:14:42.490
And we measure the high
creative people versus normals.

00:14:42.490 --> 00:14:43.960
And we notice three things about them.

00:14:43.960 --> 00:14:46.720
One, they have more visual and
more tactile awareness of the world,

00:14:46.720 --> 00:14:50.610
more parts of their brain light up
when they see and touch things.

00:14:50.610 --> 00:14:54.340
They have a high social awareness
response, which is the empathy response.

00:14:54.340 --> 00:14:56.050
Many times, high creatives are introverts.

00:14:56.050 --> 00:14:58.000
So it's not that they're social people,

00:14:58.000 --> 00:15:01.500
it's just that they notice
social interactions.

00:15:01.500 --> 00:15:04.650
They're highly empathetic
to other people's feelings.

00:15:04.650 --> 00:15:05.570
But the one that's interesting,

00:15:05.570 --> 00:15:09.850
they have what psychologists call
a low fear response to novelty.

00:15:09.850 --> 00:15:12.740
Low fear response to novelty
means they basically,

00:15:12.740 --> 00:15:15.410
when shown something they've
never seen before in a scanner,

00:15:15.410 --> 00:15:18.650
the part of their brain that should light
up and go I haven't seen this before.

00:15:18.650 --> 00:15:21.890
And the flight, fight or freeze response
should trigger, the amygdala and

00:15:21.890 --> 00:15:23.600
other parts of the brain.

00:15:23.600 --> 00:15:24.670
That just doesn't happen to them.

00:15:24.670 --> 00:15:29.900
They see something that they've never
seen before and they don't frighten.

00:15:30.930 --> 00:15:37.191
So the thing that's interesting about that
is that's a totally trainable function.

00:15:37.191 --> 00:15:39.926
You could learn low fear
response to novelty.

00:15:39.926 --> 00:15:44.831
The other word for that, by the way,
commonly held in the culture is curiosity.

00:15:44.831 --> 00:15:46.680
So instead of going what's that?

00:15:46.680 --> 00:15:48.441
They go!

00:15:48.441 --> 00:15:49.680
What's that?

00:15:49.680 --> 00:15:52.600
And then they move towards
things that are unusual.

00:15:52.600 --> 00:15:54.040
The work of Albert Bandura,

00:15:54.040 --> 00:15:58.800
this famous psychologist who teaches here
at Stanford on ridding people of phobias.

00:15:58.800 --> 00:16:01.710
This idea of a guided mastery,
take very small steps and

00:16:01.710 --> 00:16:03.890
eventually learn to overcome your phobia.

00:16:03.890 --> 00:16:07.573
Is the basis of what we teach in
brainstorming, what we teach for

00:16:07.573 --> 00:16:11.599
mind mapping, what we teach for
other creativity techniques where we

00:16:11.599 --> 00:16:15.646
train people to lower their fear of
doing something unusual or novel.

00:16:15.646 --> 00:16:19.051
Which is just baked into us,
it's an evolutionary thing that you would

00:16:19.051 --> 00:16:21.842
want to be fearful of something
that's unknown, right?

00:16:21.842 --> 00:16:24.585
You're walking through the jungle and
something jumps out at you and

00:16:24.585 --> 00:16:28.380
you can't identify it, it might eat you,
you would want to have that fear response.

00:16:28.380 --> 00:16:31.590
But we have creatives, creatives
are left in the gene pool we think,

00:16:31.590 --> 00:16:33.190
because when situations change,

00:16:33.190 --> 00:16:38.100
you need people who naturally come up with
ideas that no one else would have had.

00:16:39.270 --> 00:16:44.330
They were probably the people who
in caveman days, cave dweller days,

00:16:44.330 --> 00:16:47.910
or when everyone else was out hunting they
were painting pictures on the side of

00:16:47.910 --> 00:16:51.025
the cave and no one could quite
figure out what to do with them.

00:16:51.025 --> 00:16:51.720
You know these people.

00:16:51.720 --> 00:16:55.085
They're your friends who are artists or
poets or designers or

00:16:55.085 --> 00:16:56.425
writers or something.

00:16:56.425 --> 00:17:00.985
They often are socially awkward because
they don't pick up on the clues of society

00:17:00.985 --> 00:17:04.285
that tend to narrow our
perception of what is possible.

00:17:04.285 --> 00:17:08.255
And they retain a sort of childlike but

00:17:08.255 --> 00:17:13.020
sometimes clumsy sort of creativity
as they move through the world.

00:17:13.020 --> 00:17:14.460
So this is totally learnable.

00:17:14.460 --> 00:17:17.130
We can teach people to
lower their fear response.

00:17:17.130 --> 00:17:20.340
And to generate ideas without censoring.

00:17:20.340 --> 00:17:23.270
But my point about all this
sort of neuroscience here, and

00:17:23.270 --> 00:17:25.840
I don't want to get lost in it too much,
is that we are, in fact, in a box.

00:17:25.840 --> 00:17:29.290
This out of the box thinking
is not really possible.

00:17:29.290 --> 00:17:32.499
What is possible it to
understand a few things.

00:17:33.890 --> 00:17:34.810
The box is real.

00:17:34.810 --> 00:17:37.950
So that's just the way our brains evolved,
and there's nothing wrong with you if you

00:17:37.950 --> 00:17:40.000
feel like, hey,
I'm not as creative as the next person.

00:17:40.000 --> 00:17:40.740
That's not the issue.

00:17:42.260 --> 00:17:46.660
The creativity is
probably a brain deficit.

00:17:46.660 --> 00:17:49.430
So this thing where we scan people
who don't have fear response,

00:17:49.430 --> 00:17:53.230
that's actually a part of a circuit in
their brain that's supposed to be working.

00:17:53.230 --> 00:17:54.670
But it's broken.

00:17:54.670 --> 00:17:58.130
So that's okay, because that's actually
good news, because that means we can train

00:17:58.130 --> 00:18:02.610
that part of your brain to turn off when
you want to have access to creativity.

00:18:02.610 --> 00:18:05.190
You're actually in a better state
than a high creative person,

00:18:05.190 --> 00:18:08.312
because they don't have any
access to fear response, so

00:18:08.312 --> 00:18:12.060
they constantly bumping into things in
culture where they say the wrong thing or

00:18:12.060 --> 00:18:14.450
do the wrong thing, and
they don't understand why.

00:18:14.450 --> 00:18:19.480
You understand culture and the culture of
your community, your personal culture,

00:18:19.480 --> 00:18:21.630
the people around you, and
the culture of your company.

00:18:21.630 --> 00:18:23.770
So you can turn on and

00:18:23.770 --> 00:18:27.900
off your creativity whenever you want to,
once you're trained to do it.

00:18:27.900 --> 00:18:29.990
Creativity is a high-energy
brain behavior.

00:18:29.990 --> 00:18:33.800
Brains don't like to operate at
40 Watts and full circuits on.

00:18:33.800 --> 00:18:35.160
But you can train yourself to do that.

00:18:35.160 --> 00:18:37.270
It's just like aerobic training.

00:18:37.270 --> 00:18:40.480
And creativity therefore can be learned,
which is really important is

00:18:40.480 --> 00:18:44.840
that's what we do here at Stanford and at
the D school and in the design programs.

00:18:44.840 --> 00:18:47.100
We teach people to be more creative.

00:18:47.100 --> 00:18:48.540
So we're teaching them to
lower their fear of failure.

00:18:48.540 --> 00:18:51.530
We're teach them that the brain
organizes things by schema.

00:18:51.530 --> 00:18:52.990
So just change the schema and

00:18:52.990 --> 00:18:55.980
your brain will automatically
change the kinds of idea it has.

00:18:55.980 --> 00:18:58.950
When we're brainstorming and people get
stuck, I say, okay, now let's imagine

00:18:58.950 --> 00:19:01.980
we're on Mars, and gravity was
one-fifth the gravity of the Earth.

00:19:01.980 --> 00:19:02.900
What would we do?
And

00:19:02.900 --> 00:19:05.470
then they have a whole bunch of new ideas,
and they change the schema again,

00:19:05.470 --> 00:19:08.360
and so
now imagine that we're all elephants.

00:19:08.360 --> 00:19:11.160
We have to make this interaction
happen with our trunks.

00:19:11.160 --> 00:19:11.954
Now what will you do?

00:19:11.954 --> 00:19:16.878
And it's just a game to get people to
break out of the set they were in and

00:19:16.878 --> 00:19:17.892
try a new set.

00:19:17.892 --> 00:19:21.347
And then, all of those ideas
becomes possible in the brainstorm.

00:19:21.347 --> 00:19:22.492
And then we down select for

00:19:22.492 --> 00:19:26.120
the ones we can turn into something
that's actually actionable.

00:19:26.120 --> 00:19:28.970
And so you can access this
improvisational intelligence,

00:19:28.970 --> 00:19:30.794
this kinesthetic intelligence, and

00:19:30.794 --> 00:19:34.109
all these other things once you
understand how the box is organized.

00:19:35.927 --> 00:19:40.727
So in the, I'm going to give you three
ideas and a couple of techniques for

00:19:40.727 --> 00:19:41.610
doing this.

00:19:41.610 --> 00:19:45.420
One, the first one seems to like,
it's a little bit counterintuitive.

00:19:45.420 --> 00:19:48.390
I want you to train yourself to get stuck.

00:19:48.390 --> 00:19:51.480
I want you to put yourself in
circumstances where you get stuck

00:19:51.480 --> 00:19:52.800
over and over again.

00:19:52.800 --> 00:19:55.932
Literally, your brain freezes and
you just don't have any ideas.

00:19:55.932 --> 00:20:01.420
The one I do a lot with executives and
folks who come into

00:20:01.420 --> 00:20:06.540
the master class that we'll talk about
later is the 30 circles exercise.

00:20:06.540 --> 00:20:07.890
It's a very simple exercise.

00:20:07.890 --> 00:20:10.290
You just take a piece of paper
that's got 30 circles on it,

00:20:10.290 --> 00:20:12.310
six rows of five circles.

00:20:12.310 --> 00:20:14.610
And I say turn the circles into something.

00:20:16.000 --> 00:20:20.150
And I give him a couple of prompts
like you could make a smiley face,

00:20:20.150 --> 00:20:22.442
you could make a flower.

00:20:22.442 --> 00:20:25.195
And there's a number of
variations on this exercise.

00:20:25.195 --> 00:20:27.230
1,000 uses for a paperclip.

00:20:27.230 --> 00:20:32.953
Or pick any common object that people
have labelled as either boring or

00:20:32.953 --> 00:20:37.650
dull or not good,
like a good fodder for creativity.

00:20:37.650 --> 00:20:40.690
Take anything and say now change
this into a million other things, or

00:20:40.690 --> 00:20:41.500
a hundred other things.

00:20:42.730 --> 00:20:45.272
And people get stuck because
they drop into schemas.

00:20:45.272 --> 00:20:49.720
Well, a circle can only be face or
a circle can only be a flower, and

00:20:49.720 --> 00:20:54.330
I'm not going to talk about the ways
you soft quote solve this problem.

00:20:54.330 --> 00:20:58.230
But what happens in this exercise is they
have an experience of getting stuck and

00:20:58.230 --> 00:21:01.050
I ask them to examine what
that experience is like.

00:21:01.050 --> 00:21:02.490
Most people do have an idea.

00:21:02.490 --> 00:21:05.651
They have a what we call a a-ha moment,
when they realize, I could, I don't have

00:21:05.651 --> 00:21:08.822
to make just make flowers, I could
make baseballs and soccer balls, and.

00:21:08.822 --> 00:21:12.275
And that's a moment where their
creativity kind of bursts forward.

00:21:12.275 --> 00:21:18.860
And once you understand
the sort of the cycle of that,

00:21:18.860 --> 00:21:22.210
this mindful of process,
the getting stuck, the a-ha moment that

00:21:22.210 --> 00:21:25.830
follows right after that, then the getting
stuck again and the a-ha moment.

00:21:25.830 --> 00:21:29.180
When I scan your brain during
this exercise, when you're stuck,

00:21:29.180 --> 00:21:32.460
your flight or flight or freeze response
is triggered, your fear response.

00:21:32.460 --> 00:21:34.610
Get away, this is a dangerous problem.

00:21:34.610 --> 00:21:37.620
You're failing in front of the professor.

00:21:37.620 --> 00:21:39.400
Stop, stop, run.

00:21:39.400 --> 00:21:42.980
But when you have the ah-ha moment
your brain is generating endorphins

00:21:42.980 --> 00:21:47.360
both the left and right hemispheres
are firing in synchrony, and

00:21:47.360 --> 00:21:51.200
you're really going into
the problem in a new way.

00:21:51.200 --> 00:21:56.120
So I want you to train yourself to be
stuck and then have find the ah-ha moment,

00:21:56.120 --> 00:21:58.120
be stuck and find the ah-ha moment.

00:21:58.120 --> 00:22:02.170
The misunderstanding about designers and
this design thinking thing is that somehow

00:22:02.170 --> 00:22:05.480
we're very facile and we never get
stuck and we solve all the problems.

00:22:05.480 --> 00:22:07.870
No, in fact,
since we've decided to sign up for

00:22:07.870 --> 00:22:11.510
a career where we get a new problem
every day that we've never seen before,

00:22:11.510 --> 00:22:16.550
we almost always start stuck,
and then discover a way forward.

00:22:16.550 --> 00:22:20.840
So this learning to not be
afraid of being stuck, and

00:22:20.840 --> 00:22:23.810
learning how to find the a-ha moment,
is the first trick.

00:22:25.370 --> 00:22:28.220
Second trick, people do brainstorming.

00:22:28.220 --> 00:22:31.585
I just came back from a workshop where
I was teaching some brainstorming, and

00:22:31.585 --> 00:22:34.820
it was probably the worst brainstorming
I've ever seen people do.

00:22:34.820 --> 00:22:39.750
But we got somewhere, and we did it two or
three rounds and we got better and better.

00:22:39.750 --> 00:22:41.358
So everybody can get better.

00:22:42.430 --> 00:22:43.420
But here's the deal.

00:22:43.420 --> 00:22:45.980
Most people think about
brainstorming get in a room,

00:22:45.980 --> 00:22:49.610
somebody says let's come up with bunch
of ideas that improve something.

00:22:49.610 --> 00:22:51.140
And we make,

00:22:51.140 --> 00:22:56.290
we all talk at once and we put some things
on the board and then we think it's done.

00:22:56.290 --> 00:23:00.993
I want you to create a new metaphor in
your mind for a brainstorming group.

00:23:00.993 --> 00:23:02.739
I want you to think about music.

00:23:02.739 --> 00:23:04.586
I want you to think about jazz.

00:23:04.586 --> 00:23:09.020
Jazz is the one form of music where
musicians agree to get together and

00:23:09.020 --> 00:23:10.644
play a song we've all played before.

00:23:10.644 --> 00:23:16.540
But while we play that song, to make
music none of us have ever heard before.

00:23:16.540 --> 00:23:19.290
We're going to improvise on that game.

00:23:19.290 --> 00:23:23.370
But when it really works,
take that to some place completely new.

00:23:23.370 --> 00:23:26.380
So this ability to
improvise in the moment,

00:23:26.380 --> 00:23:27.960
that's what brainstorming is really about.

00:23:29.490 --> 00:23:33.510
Now, you wouldn't expect to be able
to sit in with a great jazz ensemble.

00:23:33.510 --> 00:23:38.110
Miles Davis and the Kind of Blue is
the best-selling jazz album of all time.

00:23:38.110 --> 00:23:42.010
You wouldn't expect to sit in
if you were a beginner, right?

00:23:42.010 --> 00:23:43.670
If I said, here, here's your saxophone,

00:23:43.670 --> 00:23:46.800
go play with these guys over here
who have been playing for 30 years.

00:23:46.800 --> 00:23:49.080
You would know you were out of place.

00:23:49.080 --> 00:23:52.160
You would know that it's not,
that's not appropriate.

00:23:52.160 --> 00:23:54.303
I didn't understand why people,
when they get together and

00:23:54.303 --> 00:23:58.500
they have much practice with brainstorming
think, we're going to do this really well.

00:23:58.500 --> 00:23:59.560
You have to practice.

00:23:59.560 --> 00:24:02.611
And at first the mechanics,
just the simple mechanics of everyone

00:24:02.611 --> 00:24:05.600
having an idea,
listening to each to each other.

00:24:05.600 --> 00:24:09.540
When you, the solo in the brainstorm
is when you present an idea and

00:24:09.540 --> 00:24:11.170
everybody else is supporting you.

00:24:11.170 --> 00:24:15.260
But passing the lead back and forth is
letting someone else present an idea.

00:24:15.260 --> 00:24:18.190
So the mechanics of just getting
that done are quite difficult.

00:24:19.190 --> 00:24:22.660
But I notice when I'm working with people,
let's say I have a team of five and

00:24:22.660 --> 00:24:24.500
I say hey, Ronny.

00:24:24.500 --> 00:24:27.630
Do you think you could have
just one idea every minute?

00:24:27.630 --> 00:24:28.640
Like every 60 seconds,

00:24:28.640 --> 00:24:32.350
let's say we're trying to come
up with new ways to do webinars.

00:24:32.350 --> 00:24:35.810
Could you have one idea,
one idea a minute, that's easy.

00:24:35.810 --> 00:24:39.530
But then I go, well, there's five people
on the team, so that's one idea a minute

00:24:39.530 --> 00:24:44.920
times five times five, we should have had
25 ideas, and this team only had seven.

00:24:44.920 --> 00:24:49.150
So clearly, the mechanics of
learning to listen to each other and

00:24:49.150 --> 00:24:51.630
passing the lead back and
forth are a bit tricky.

00:24:52.650 --> 00:24:55.330
But the mechanics alone do not get
you to that place where you've

00:24:55.330 --> 00:24:56.790
practiced on your instrument.

00:24:56.790 --> 00:24:57.970
You're bringing long enough and

00:24:57.970 --> 00:25:02.728
hard enough so that you can actually
then let go in the moment and create.

00:25:02.728 --> 00:25:08.686
It's this 10,000-hour problem from
Malcolm Gladwell's stuff on creativity and

00:25:08.686 --> 00:25:10.900
on high-performance people.

00:25:10.900 --> 00:25:15.500
Now, there's some controversy
about whether 10,000

00:25:15.500 --> 00:25:19.190
hours is actually a real metric or not.

00:25:19.190 --> 00:25:21.870
But let's just take that as a metaphor,
that it's

00:25:21.870 --> 00:25:24.970
going to take you 100 hours to move from
just, I don't know what I'm doing to okay,

00:25:24.970 --> 00:25:29.400
I understand the rules and I understand
how this piece of music plays.

00:25:29.400 --> 00:25:33.120
It's going to take maybe another
1,000 hours to get to the sort

00:25:33.120 --> 00:25:36.390
of expert on this thing,
some competency, the expert.

00:25:36.390 --> 00:25:41.460
And I do think it takes many, many, many
years of practice to learn to put your

00:25:41.460 --> 00:25:47.530
brain in this frictionless state where all
the ideas that available are possible.

00:25:47.530 --> 00:25:50.840
And that where you can move from schema
to schema to schema almost without

00:25:50.840 --> 00:25:54.810
knowing it, and
constantly generate new ideas.

00:25:54.810 --> 00:25:57.690
So this inventing in the moment
is really a master skill,

00:25:57.690 --> 00:26:01.410
and it comes only after a lot of practice.

00:26:01.410 --> 00:26:03.960
So if you're having brainstorming
sessions that are less

00:26:03.960 --> 00:26:06.000
Productive than you think they should be.

00:26:06.000 --> 00:26:08.890
And I've used that metric
of one idea a minute

00:26:08.890 --> 00:26:11.760
times another minute times
the number of people.

00:26:11.760 --> 00:26:16.920
That's a novice brainstorm,
a pretty good brainstorm

00:26:16.920 --> 00:26:22.045
of people have done this before except
we will easily be able to double that.

00:26:22.045 --> 00:26:26.590
An idea every 30 seconds is not fast,
and that would generate

00:26:26.590 --> 00:26:30.560
50 ideas from a team of 5 and if you
tripled that, it would generate 75 ideas.

00:26:30.560 --> 00:26:33.400
And I have student teams
who practiced on this and

00:26:33.400 --> 00:26:38.360
I have expert teams with senior designs
who practiced on this who can easily

00:26:38.360 --> 00:26:42.380
with a team of five have a 100 to 150
ideas in five minutes, that's mastery.

00:26:42.380 --> 00:26:44.840
If your teams are nowhere
near that number yet.

00:26:45.840 --> 00:26:47.780
Just practice, it's just practice.

00:26:47.780 --> 00:26:51.920
It takes a long time to learn play
that saxophone that is your brain and

00:26:51.920 --> 00:26:54.420
you know, you're going to have to

00:26:54.420 --> 00:26:58.580
put in the hours because I don't
know any other way to do it.

00:26:58.580 --> 00:27:02.000
And you know,
get in the 10,000 hours to mastery.

00:27:02.000 --> 00:27:06.890
Okay, it's also true that
it's a mind body problem.

00:27:06.890 --> 00:27:10.320
Is getting into this state of
relaxed tension and flow so

00:27:10.320 --> 00:27:12.990
that you just have ideas over and
over again.

00:27:12.990 --> 00:27:18.400
You gotta go from the analytic side of
your brain to you synthesis side of your

00:27:18.400 --> 00:27:22.030
brain and you don't have to necessarily
sit in a full lotus position but you have

00:27:22.030 --> 00:27:26.020
to be relaxed and here's the other thing,
when people do these brainstorming stuff

00:27:26.020 --> 00:27:30.030
in companies and teams,
they just come from a budget meeting.

00:27:30.030 --> 00:27:32.520
They just come from a negotiation or
something.

00:27:32.520 --> 00:27:34.200
They're totally in their left brain,
and so

00:27:34.200 --> 00:27:37.830
they need some opportunity
to sort of warm up.

00:27:37.830 --> 00:27:41.780
Quiet down the prefrontal cortex,
excite the right brain and

00:27:41.780 --> 00:27:46.870
the cognitive processes that are there,
and context and meaning and emotion.

00:27:46.870 --> 00:27:49.530
because all of those things
are going to result in better ideas.

00:27:49.530 --> 00:27:53.320
So in order to do that,
you have to warm up.

00:27:53.320 --> 00:27:57.420
What we use at the D School
is a lot of improv games.

00:27:58.800 --> 00:28:00.970
A fantastic book by Patricia Ryan Madson,

00:28:00.970 --> 00:28:05.410
who was the professor of improv here
in the drama department here for

00:28:05.410 --> 00:28:09.740
30 years and was considered one of
the most extraordinary teachers

00:28:09.740 --> 00:28:12.775
on campus she wrote a book called “Improv
Wisdom: Don't Prepare Just Show Up”.

00:28:13.890 --> 00:28:18.880
And we have tones of her improv
stuff from improv theater,

00:28:18.880 --> 00:28:23.025
tradition games called Sound Ball where
you're throwing around imaginary balls

00:28:23.025 --> 00:28:25.950
Proverbs, you're making imaginary poems,
and

00:28:25.950 --> 00:28:30.880
then this notion that they have in improv
of no matter what just happened on stage,

00:28:30.880 --> 00:28:34.720
the only thing you're
allowed to say is yes.

00:28:34.720 --> 00:28:36.100
And build on that to be the next thing.

00:28:36.100 --> 00:28:40.240
If you're looking for
a bunch of improv games we put up a bunch.

00:28:40.240 --> 00:28:44.930
We've got a whole appendix
from Designing Your Life book.

00:28:44.930 --> 00:28:50.690
It's up on our site,
www.designingyour.life is the URL,

00:28:50.690 --> 00:28:55.660
and you can go download a copy of
all improvs we use at the D School,

00:28:55.660 --> 00:28:57.960
about 20 different games that are there.

00:28:57.960 --> 00:29:00.060
And they're broken into
sort of low impact,

00:29:00.060 --> 00:29:03.970
high impact Improv to stoke up
the team and get them excited.

00:29:03.970 --> 00:29:07.050
Improv to get the team
communicating better.

00:29:07.050 --> 00:29:12.770
But we highly suggest
improvisation as a way of building

00:29:12.770 --> 00:29:16.720
that sort of improv muscle,
that will then lead to higher creativity.

00:29:17.800 --> 00:29:21.200
The other thing about brain
storms is people tend to

00:29:21.200 --> 00:29:24.100
stop after they've looked over the ideas,
and that's not when you end.

00:29:24.100 --> 00:29:28.350
You end with the idea of selection,
sort of framing and naming the ideas and

00:29:28.350 --> 00:29:31.280
bringing them back to reality
in a way that's actionable.

00:29:31.280 --> 00:29:35.520
If you choose around the usual criteria,
can we do this?

00:29:35.520 --> 00:29:36.830
Is it feasible?

00:29:36.830 --> 00:29:38.380
You'll get pretty incremental results.

00:29:38.380 --> 00:29:41.380
We suggest you choose around potential and

00:29:41.380 --> 00:29:45.040
pick words that excite
emotional connections.

00:29:45.040 --> 00:29:49.640
Like what's the most delightful idea,
what's the most abstract idea?

00:29:49.640 --> 00:29:50.470
What's the idea?

00:29:50.470 --> 00:29:55.170
If we could avoid one law of physics,
gravity, or

00:29:55.170 --> 00:29:59.560
one law of economics, like profit
What would be the most exciting idea?

00:29:59.560 --> 00:30:04.920
And that way you'll not limit your
access to being able to frame

00:30:04.920 --> 00:30:07.160
stuff that turns out to
be really actionable.

00:30:08.590 --> 00:30:11.760
So here's the deal, brainstorming will
absolutely transform you, as a person,

00:30:11.760 --> 00:30:13.680
because it changes your behavior.

00:30:13.680 --> 00:30:19.770
The data shows that novices brainstorm
poorly, and great brainstorming

00:30:19.770 --> 00:30:22.330
is like great jazz you have to be
expert enough to create in the moment.

00:30:22.330 --> 00:30:26.560
That comes from practice and then you need
to improve your output by warming up with

00:30:26.560 --> 00:30:30.240
improve games and selecting better
when you get to the end for

00:30:30.240 --> 00:30:31.910
the ideas that have the most potential.

00:30:34.060 --> 00:30:39.320
Third idea is space, not this kind
of space but this kind of space.

00:30:39.320 --> 00:30:42.940
When you walk into a room that looks like
this, and most of you have been students

00:30:42.940 --> 00:30:46.300
probably at some point in your life
realize I know what I'm supposed to do.

00:30:46.300 --> 00:30:50.770
I'm supposed to sit down, shut up and
be passive receiver of information, i.e.,

00:30:50.770 --> 00:30:52.910
someone's going to lecture at me.

00:30:52.910 --> 00:30:56.170
In a space like this,
you cannot be creative.

00:30:56.170 --> 00:30:58.190
My friends over at IDEO
have a great saying,

00:30:58.190 --> 00:31:00.339
also when you think about
most corporate spaces.

00:31:01.500 --> 00:31:05.150
Little cubicles that people sit in and
they're all divided from each other and

00:31:05.150 --> 00:31:06.530
they can't see each other.

00:31:06.530 --> 00:31:10.320
They say if you live in a cubicle world,
you will think cubicle thoughts, little,

00:31:10.320 --> 00:31:12.160
tiny square thoughts.

00:31:12.160 --> 00:31:14.530
You gotta get out of
those kinds of spaces.

00:31:14.530 --> 00:31:16.680
This is the kind of space we work in.

00:31:16.680 --> 00:31:21.430
This is one of the studios at
the D School, everything's on wheels,

00:31:21.430 --> 00:31:23.030
it's a very collaborative space.

00:31:23.030 --> 00:31:28.750
It's quite colorful, we have tools around
for instantaneous brainstorming sessions,

00:31:28.750 --> 00:31:31.820
and also for
instantaneous prototyping sessions.

00:31:31.820 --> 00:31:34.630
Raw, there's been a bunch of studies.

00:31:34.630 --> 00:31:38.316
There was a Harvard study of
36,000 scientific papers,

00:31:38.316 --> 00:31:40.270
all ranked on innovation.

00:31:40.270 --> 00:31:44.170
And the two significant things
that they noticed about

00:31:44.170 --> 00:31:48.980
the most innovative papers is
that innovators were co-located.

00:31:48.980 --> 00:31:52.070
In other words,
this idea of radical collaboration.

00:31:52.070 --> 00:31:56.110
People from different parts of the
organization were all sitting together,

00:31:56.110 --> 00:31:58.870
and the fact that they had really
different backgrounds, and really

00:31:58.870 --> 00:32:03.850
different ways of framing the ideas,
tended to juice up the innovation content.

00:32:03.850 --> 00:32:08.610
And that they mostly worked in raw or
easily modified environments.

00:32:08.610 --> 00:32:11.380
There was a famous building,
Building 20 at MIT.

00:32:11.380 --> 00:32:16.000
Which was constructed as a temporary
building just around World War II for

00:32:16.000 --> 00:32:20.870
some advanced research that MIT
thanked us for doing for the military.

00:32:20.870 --> 00:32:26.100
And it was just a space that nobody
paid any attention to or took care of.

00:32:26.100 --> 00:32:31.150
And here you'll hear it was just this
crappy building that people who could not

00:32:31.150 --> 00:32:36.320
get good space at MIT were assigned and it
turns out that that is where all the big

00:32:36.320 --> 00:32:40.430
innovations came and
the patents came from the MIT environment.

00:32:40.430 --> 00:32:45.880
So Raw space, this idea of space creating
behaviour, is such a powerful metaphor.

00:32:45.880 --> 00:32:47.290
We suggest,

00:32:47.290 --> 00:32:50.860
if you want to be more creative,
be in a more creative environment.

00:32:50.860 --> 00:32:53.930
Create sort of like this war room, or
this project room place for yourself.

00:32:53.930 --> 00:32:55.860
Even if you do it at home for

00:32:55.860 --> 00:33:00.090
your own personal projects, make the
things that you're working on persistent.

00:33:00.090 --> 00:33:01.150
Put up the pic.

00:33:01.150 --> 00:33:02.970
It's like a physical Pinterest.

00:33:02.970 --> 00:33:05.400
Pick pictures of everything
that you're thinking about.

00:33:05.400 --> 00:33:07.240
Put the pictures all up all the time.

00:33:07.240 --> 00:33:11.490
Make sure that when you walk back in
to that room the information is the and

00:33:11.490 --> 00:33:14.450
is crewing your subconscious
to think again.

00:33:14.450 --> 00:33:19.680
Remove all the status clues, status and
hierarchy defeats creativity and

00:33:19.680 --> 00:33:23.050
the d.school rooms there is no visible
hierarchy either, there's no stage,

00:33:23.050 --> 00:33:25.310
there's no front,
there's no back to the room,

00:33:25.310 --> 00:33:27.810
your rooms are completely self-authoring.

00:33:27.810 --> 00:33:28.970
Everything's on wheels.

00:33:28.970 --> 00:33:31.890
Everything can be hacked,
and nothing is precious.

00:33:31.890 --> 00:33:36.320
So people believe when they walk into
these spaces that we're demanding their

00:33:36.320 --> 00:33:39.520
creativity because their creativity
has to frame what the room looks like.

00:33:40.810 --> 00:33:43.110
It's such a powerful, and
such a simple thing to do.

00:33:43.110 --> 00:33:45.000
Just change your space.

00:33:45.000 --> 00:33:48.589
Couple of guys, Scott Doorley and Scott
Witthoft wrote a book from the D School

00:33:48.589 --> 00:33:54.030
called “Make Space,” which is kind of
the go-to reference for people who want to

00:33:54.030 --> 00:33:58.220
try to either create more creative space
at home or creative space where they work.

00:33:58.220 --> 00:34:00.040
It's such an important thing.

00:34:00.040 --> 00:34:03.370
All right, so here's the takeaways.

00:34:03.370 --> 00:34:06.780
Changing, if you want to
become more creative.

00:34:06.780 --> 00:34:08.240
Then something has to change, right.

00:34:08.240 --> 00:34:10.180
Something is going to change.

00:34:10.180 --> 00:34:12.890
Now some people think you just read a
bunch of books and you think about it and

00:34:12.890 --> 00:34:14.050
then you'll be more creative.

00:34:14.050 --> 00:34:15.630
There's no evidence that that's true.

00:34:15.630 --> 00:34:19.090
What is true is that if you
change your behavior you

00:34:20.230 --> 00:34:22.410
will be indistinguishable
from a creative person.

00:34:22.410 --> 00:34:25.320
I.e., if you start brainstorming,

00:34:25.320 --> 00:34:30.276
if you are able to change
your schemas quickly

00:34:30.276 --> 00:34:35.560
If you start developing a visual language,
you sketch a lot and you doodle a lot.

00:34:35.560 --> 00:34:40.948
If you are the kind of person
who's more likely to say 'yes and

00:34:40.948 --> 00:34:45.060
-' when someone has an idea instead of
'well, wait a minute, but that won't work.

00:34:45.060 --> 00:34:49.840
If you can just change the mode of
the way you interact to a 'yes and' mode

00:34:51.170 --> 00:34:54.450
You will one, have better ideas, two,
they will be more creative ideas and

00:34:54.450 --> 00:34:56.300
three, whether you feel creative or

00:34:56.300 --> 00:34:59.650
not you will be indistinguishable
from a creative person.

00:34:59.650 --> 00:35:03.280
Because in our model creativity
is a series of behaviors and

00:35:03.280 --> 00:35:06.870
by the way the behaviors, of course,
change you neurologically.

00:35:06.870 --> 00:35:11.170
We studied one class, the class they
teach called the Designing Your Life

00:35:11.170 --> 00:35:12.640
where students from all
over the university,

00:35:12.640 --> 00:35:17.480
that are not in a creative major, they can
be in econ or history or whatever major,

00:35:17.480 --> 00:35:20.400
they come into class with
sheets from these principles.

00:35:20.400 --> 00:35:24.120
And we have measured an increase
in their ability to ideate from

00:35:24.120 --> 00:35:27.700
the beginning of the class to the end and
what we're going to start doing next is

00:35:27.700 --> 00:35:30.050
trying to see if we've actually
built new brain circuits.

00:35:30.050 --> 00:35:32.850
We're going to scan them before and
after class.

00:35:32.850 --> 00:35:35.780
But my theory is that this
behaviours are certainly going to

00:35:35.780 --> 00:35:39.700
create new circuits in the brain
which will allow people to be

00:35:39.700 --> 00:35:41.710
experience their life
had been more creative.

00:35:41.710 --> 00:35:46.860
So, three powerful things get stuck more
often, challenge yourself with problems

00:35:46.860 --> 00:35:51.000
you don't know how to solve them, wait for
and then work hard to get the AHA moment.

00:35:51.000 --> 00:35:53.650
Move to this jazz model
of brainstorming and

00:35:53.650 --> 00:35:55.910
create a space that demands new behaviors.

00:35:55.910 --> 00:35:59.760
And then if you want to be this person,
one you have to act differently,

00:35:59.760 --> 00:36:00.980
you have to act this way.

00:36:00.980 --> 00:36:02.950
And you want to probably
reward yourself for

00:36:02.950 --> 00:36:06.340
taking these kinds of risks and
certainly if you're managing team that

00:36:06.340 --> 00:36:10.300
you want more creative behaviors from You
have to change their rewards as well.

00:36:10.300 --> 00:36:13.740
Because most of the ideas that you
have as a creative person never

00:36:13.740 --> 00:36:14.710
get realized in the world.

00:36:14.710 --> 00:36:19.090
They're just part of creating
an environment, a culture of ideas,

00:36:19.090 --> 00:36:21.100
a culture of creativity.

00:36:21.100 --> 00:36:23.695
And in So,
you have to be able to reward people for

00:36:23.695 --> 00:36:26.975
something other than
their direct production.

00:36:26.975 --> 00:36:29.405
And, by the way, be patient.

00:36:29.405 --> 00:36:33.035
To change behavior, I'm told from
the psychologists over in our psych

00:36:33.035 --> 00:36:36.535
department, people who work on behavior
change to establish a new pattern or

00:36:36.535 --> 00:36:37.845
habit takes at least, eight weeks.

00:36:38.855 --> 00:36:40.975
And, it takes eight weeks
of concentrated focus.

00:36:40.975 --> 00:36:43.080
It can't just be sort of, a side game.

00:36:43.080 --> 00:36:45.930
And most of us don't
have the time to do that.

00:36:45.930 --> 00:36:47.070
So be patient with yourself.

00:36:47.070 --> 00:36:51.450
It may take a while to have these
new mindsets of curiosity and

00:36:51.450 --> 00:36:53.970
radical collaboration, and reframing.

00:36:53.970 --> 00:36:57.280
And better brainstorming and new spaces.

00:36:57.280 --> 00:37:00.240
It may take a while for
that to take effect.

00:37:00.240 --> 00:37:04.870
But once it does, you'll start discovering
that the world is a much more.

00:37:04.870 --> 00:37:10.000
Interesting place and that your
curiosity will pull into really new and

00:37:10.000 --> 00:37:14.720
interesting problems in a way that you
probably could not have predicted.

00:37:15.930 --> 00:37:19.740
I always end the works that we do here,
because it is about changing people's

00:37:19.740 --> 00:37:23.340
behavior, with a Gandhi quote, be
the change you want to see in the world.

00:37:24.680 --> 00:37:28.250
You model these behaviors for
yourself, for your family, for

00:37:28.250 --> 00:37:32.820
your kids Particularly so
important to remodeling the attitudes and

00:37:32.820 --> 00:37:37.610
the yes and optimism of a creative
person for children, because they get so

00:37:37.610 --> 00:37:41.630
little creative stimulation
unfortunately in our education system.

00:37:41.630 --> 00:37:46.110
And also at work people will
notice you have changed, and

00:37:46.110 --> 00:37:51.140
by changing you will change the world and
with that I'd like to turn it back

00:37:51.140 --> 00:37:56.480
to Bill to take a few questions
from you in the time that we have.

00:37:56.480 --> 00:38:00.820
So, Bill one question that we received,
a person was asking.

00:38:00.820 --> 00:38:04.070
Is it better to practice
brainstorming on kind of

00:38:04.070 --> 00:38:07.690
real challenges that you have at work or
as part of your job or

00:38:07.690 --> 00:38:11.060
whether it’s better to start
practicing on model fake?

00:38:12.600 --> 00:38:15.360
Sample problems,
which one is more effective?

00:38:16.690 --> 00:38:19.460
&gt;&gt; Well, I think if you're
going to follow your curiosity,

00:38:19.460 --> 00:38:22.520
you want to be working on problems
that you are interested in.

00:38:22.520 --> 00:38:27.360
And when we We do workshops and stuff,
we have a bunch of model problems

00:38:27.360 --> 00:38:30.120
we designed that have all
sorts of little tricks and

00:38:30.120 --> 00:38:35.750
traps in them in ways of getting people
stuck in their schematic thinking.

00:38:35.750 --> 00:38:37.570
If you're just trying to practice and

00:38:37.570 --> 00:38:41.110
build up your skill,
working on stuff that you have some

00:38:42.290 --> 00:38:45.070
Stake in some emotional stake in
the outcome is probably a good idea.

00:38:45.070 --> 00:38:47.100
So real problems at work.

00:38:47.100 --> 00:38:49.380
Assuming that you care about work and

00:38:49.380 --> 00:38:53.190
the things that you're working on
would be excellent ways to do that.

00:38:53.190 --> 00:38:58.420
And also,
just think about when you come home and

00:38:58.420 --> 00:39:00.630
you guys are just trying
to figure out like.

00:39:00.630 --> 00:39:04.280
We can't ever seem to keep this awful
schedule straight and we keep missing,

00:39:04.280 --> 00:39:05.880
we're always late for soccer games.

00:39:05.880 --> 00:39:08.940
How could we brainstorm 100 different ways

00:39:08.940 --> 00:39:13.240
to make sure that we always pick going
to soccer games or whatever other family

00:39:14.580 --> 00:39:18.850
challenges you have
are solved in a creative way.

00:39:18.850 --> 00:39:21.870
And that would also include
probably bring your kids or

00:39:21.870 --> 00:39:24.000
other people into their
brainstorming methodology.

00:39:24.000 --> 00:39:28.600
And you might learn something from their
ability to be really non-linear and

00:39:28.600 --> 00:39:29.690
open in thinking.

00:39:29.690 --> 00:39:34.930
So, I think, I guess make sure to answer
is pick something you actually care about.

00:39:34.930 --> 00:39:36.570
And I'm assuming you care
about the problems at work,

00:39:36.570 --> 00:39:38.140
those would be great to brainstorm.

00:39:38.140 --> 00:39:42.260
But pick things that your
curiosity pulls you towards, and

00:39:42.260 --> 00:39:44.300
you'll do a better job because
you'll have a stake in the outcome.

00:39:45.860 --> 00:39:49.600
&gt;&gt; So a couple of questions came
up about the space question.

00:39:49.600 --> 00:39:53.410
People were intrigued about using
space to further creativity.

00:39:53.410 --> 00:39:58.160
One question had to do with, how do
you remove status symbols [INAUDIBLE]?

00:39:58.160 --> 00:40:04.400
And if you can say more about that
As well as the question around this

00:40:04.400 --> 00:40:08.870
idea of co-location, the importance of
having people close together physically,

00:40:08.870 --> 00:40:12.280
does that in any way interfere
with the fence of boundaries or

00:40:12.280 --> 00:40:15.990
safety that people need in
order to be creative and open.

00:40:15.990 --> 00:40:21.359
&gt;&gt; Okay,
well I think that the first one first,

00:40:21.359 --> 00:40:24.285
it's
&gt;&gt; The idea, I mean, if you think about,

00:40:24.285 --> 00:40:28.385
artists always work in studios
typically surrounded by other artists.

00:40:28.385 --> 00:40:31.335
Designers work in a studio mode.

00:40:31.335 --> 00:40:35.150
Well, if you go over to the IDEO offices,
and I'll just use IDEO as an example

00:40:35.150 --> 00:40:40.050
as one of the most creative, innovative
strategy and product organizations around.

00:40:40.050 --> 00:40:44.453
Also founded by one of our professors,
David Kelly.

00:40:44.453 --> 00:40:46.299
[LAUGH] But
David came out of this program.

00:40:46.299 --> 00:40:48.990
He was a graduate student
when I was a senior.

00:40:48.990 --> 00:40:53.290
And, it's basically IDEO as
an example of this kind of thinking.

00:40:53.290 --> 00:40:57.470
They always formed project rooms,
where people have their own office, but

00:40:57.470 --> 00:41:00.780
when they're working on the project,
they're in the project room together.

00:41:00.780 --> 00:41:05.360
And that allows them to minimize when you
are on the project team you are just on

00:41:05.360 --> 00:41:06.130
the project team.

00:41:06.130 --> 00:41:11.060
There's a project coordinator or lead,
but they don't have managerial authority

00:41:11.060 --> 00:41:15.430
over anything other than the project,
the schedule, and keeping things moving.

00:41:16.500 --> 00:41:20.900
It's not unusual to find artists and
musicians and

00:41:20.900 --> 00:41:23.410
people working together
collaborating in these groups.

00:41:23.410 --> 00:41:24.490
Musicians get together for

00:41:24.490 --> 00:41:28.220
jam sessions You know,
artists get together for critique.

00:41:28.220 --> 00:41:32.830
So, this idea of forming a space
where that kind of behavior is really

00:41:32.830 --> 00:41:37.220
demanded and
accepted is what the space piece is about.

00:41:37.220 --> 00:41:42.322
The idea of co-adaptation and
safety- That's interesting,

00:41:42.322 --> 00:41:48.050
I never thought of collaborating
with other people as being unsafe.

00:41:48.050 --> 00:41:50.860
But I could imagine in some work
environments that's the way

00:41:50.860 --> 00:41:53.250
things had been set up.

00:41:53.250 --> 00:41:58.760
Because in fact the team has been
pulled together from different

00:41:58.760 --> 00:42:01.780
organizations, different
parts of the organization.

00:42:01.780 --> 00:42:05.596
Really all have to individually go back to
the other part of the organization they're

00:42:05.596 --> 00:42:08.252
from to get permission to do
what the team has decided to do.

00:42:08.252 --> 00:42:11.454
And so that's an inherently unsafe place
because the team that's going to come

00:42:11.454 --> 00:42:12.742
up with a bunch of great ideas and

00:42:12.742 --> 00:42:16.110
then everyone's going to have to go back
to their boss and find out if that's okay.

00:42:17.110 --> 00:42:21.880
So you know, if you're in that
kind of an environment we need

00:42:21.880 --> 00:42:26.650
to work probably more on how do
we structure these teams, and

00:42:26.650 --> 00:42:31.156
how are the teams empowered
to take on these challenges.

00:42:31.156 --> 00:42:34.033
In some companies,
there's sort of a false promise made,

00:42:34.033 --> 00:42:35.940
like we're going to form a task force.

00:42:35.940 --> 00:42:38.770
And people are going to get together,
and they're going to solve this problem.

00:42:38.770 --> 00:42:42.876
But in fact, what's really going on
is that people were put on the task

00:42:42.876 --> 00:42:46.093
force because they weren't
vital to other projects.

00:42:46.093 --> 00:42:49.242
Or the task force has not been
empowered to make anything

00:42:49.242 --> 00:42:53.398
other than recommendations, all of
which get sort of ignored at the end.

00:42:53.398 --> 00:42:56.904
And so you do want to be careful
in terms of your own safety

00:42:56.904 --> 00:42:59.441
in an organization to find out whether or

00:42:59.441 --> 00:43:04.020
not the projects you've been
assigned to is essentially real.

00:43:04.020 --> 00:43:07.680
I'll give you a very simple
way of measuring that.

00:43:07.680 --> 00:43:10.860
And this comes actually not from our work,
but

00:43:10.860 --> 00:43:14.080
from some other people
who studied this problem.

00:43:14.080 --> 00:43:17.720
Bob Sutton is one the guys here at
Stanford who studies this stuff.

00:43:17.720 --> 00:43:21.500
Here's a good way to find out if
the team you're on is actually

00:43:21.500 --> 00:43:24.369
empowered to make some decisions and
solve the problem.

00:43:25.560 --> 00:43:29.012
Got a team of people and
you want to go talk to some customers, or

00:43:29.012 --> 00:43:32.544
talk to a user or you just want to
go do some research some place.

00:43:32.544 --> 00:43:36.720
Are you allowed to travel without getting
permission from somebody somewhere else?

00:43:37.740 --> 00:43:40.992
So I mean I'm not talking first class
travel to Europe or something but

00:43:40.992 --> 00:43:43.138
can you fly down to LA and
meet with an expert and

00:43:43.138 --> 00:43:45.914
fly back without having to go
through some vice president.

00:43:45.914 --> 00:43:50.273
Approval, because if you can't
authorize a $500 ticket,

00:43:50.273 --> 00:43:55.590
you're not an empowered team,
it's just that really easy measure.

00:43:55.590 --> 00:43:59.321
And it's interesting because, a lot of
these organizations have all of these

00:43:59.321 --> 00:44:03.048
rules about travel and who can do it and
who can't do it and that kind of stuff And

00:44:03.048 --> 00:44:05.670
they're all designed around control and
authority.

00:44:05.670 --> 00:44:09.944
And so a team that actually has
the authority to travel and investigate

00:44:09.944 --> 00:44:14.590
a problem any way they think is important
is actually a very empowered team.

00:44:18.125 --> 00:44:22.818
Great, so a question around how you
decide who to put together on a team for

00:44:22.818 --> 00:44:26.392
a successful idealization or
brainstorming process.

00:44:26.392 --> 00:44:30.727
What are some key things that one should
think about as we're putting together

00:44:30.727 --> 00:44:31.455
such a team?

00:44:31.455 --> 00:44:35.045
And what do you think about
clients getting involved

00:44:35.045 --> 00:44:38.069
in the process when you're trying to
come up with a solution for that client?

00:44:39.500 --> 00:44:44.080
Well the idea of Radical Collaboration
is sort of how we form teams.

00:44:44.080 --> 00:44:47.400
And to think about,
go back to music analogy,

00:44:47.400 --> 00:44:49.750
we don't want six saxophone players.

00:44:49.750 --> 00:44:50.677
We need a saxophone player.

00:44:50.677 --> 00:44:51.420
We need a trumpet player.

00:44:51.420 --> 00:44:52.297
Maybe we need a drummer.

00:44:52.297 --> 00:44:53.166
We need a bass.

00:44:53.166 --> 00:44:56.570
So we need people from different
disciplines that have some bearing or

00:44:56.570 --> 00:44:59.530
insight on the problem we're working.

00:44:59.530 --> 00:45:02.780
And that's tricky, because everybody's
coming from a different point of view, and

00:45:02.780 --> 00:45:04.350
not everybody knows how to play together.

00:45:04.350 --> 00:45:09.280
And so you might need to do some warm ups,
and some structured exercises

00:45:09.280 --> 00:45:13.620
around how we're going to play together,
and exchange information.

00:45:13.620 --> 00:45:18.180
I do think it's often really insightful
to bring the customer's voice in, whoever

00:45:18.180 --> 00:45:21.800
that customer is, if you're a consulting
person, you're working with a client.

00:45:21.800 --> 00:45:22.930
The client's the customer, but

00:45:22.930 --> 00:45:25.850
the client always has their problem they
want you to solve for their business.

00:45:25.850 --> 00:45:27.485
So they have a customer too.

00:45:27.485 --> 00:45:30.608
So it's not always not only
the client that we bring in,

00:45:30.608 --> 00:45:34.211
because the client may actually
suppress the conversation in a way.

00:45:34.211 --> 00:45:38.327
Because their concern is that they want to
get a project done efficiently and quickly

00:45:38.327 --> 00:45:42.800
and stuff that a brainstorm can look
like an inefficient part of the process.

00:45:42.800 --> 00:45:44.590
But going directly to the customer,

00:45:44.590 --> 00:45:48.560
the user that you want to engage with,
that's often very powerful.

00:45:49.670 --> 00:45:53.760
I was seven years at Apple working on
the new, the first generation of laptops,

00:45:53.760 --> 00:45:56.280
and a bunch of really
innovative stuff there.

00:45:56.280 --> 00:45:57.780
Apple's known for that innovation.

00:45:57.780 --> 00:46:02.400
And people say Apple doesn't do market
research But that's not actually true.

00:46:02.400 --> 00:46:05.218
Apple does more customer
research than anyone I know.

00:46:05.218 --> 00:46:07.590
We used to, we used to.

00:46:07.590 --> 00:46:11.090
Hundreds of hours of standing behind
a one-way mirror watching people try to,

00:46:11.090 --> 00:46:13.720
you know, get a laptop to send an email.

00:46:13.720 --> 00:46:17.395
And all the confusion about software and
what's the operating system.

00:46:17.395 --> 00:46:21.587
And what's an application to try
to make computers super easy,

00:46:21.587 --> 00:46:23.770
the computers for the rest of us.

00:46:23.770 --> 00:46:25.670
That anyone can use, kids could use,
teachers could use, and

00:46:25.670 --> 00:46:27.800
they could set up their own networks.

00:46:27.800 --> 00:46:32.250
Was really hard,
because software is hard to make simple.

00:46:32.250 --> 00:46:34.270
And we used to test, and
test, and test, and test.

00:46:34.270 --> 00:46:39.930
So we were always bringing the customer
and the user into the experience.

00:46:39.930 --> 00:46:41.160
And sometimes into brainstorms.

00:46:41.160 --> 00:46:44.460
because customers don't really know
what they want or what's next for them.

00:46:44.460 --> 00:46:48.669
There's a famous Henry Ford quote that
if I'd asked people what they wanted,

00:46:48.669 --> 00:46:50.659
they would have said faster horses.

00:46:50.659 --> 00:46:54.195
By the way, there's no evidence
that Henry ever said that but

00:46:54.195 --> 00:46:57.331
it's such a snarky thing
that he probably could've.

00:46:57.331 --> 00:47:00.456
So customers don't know what they want,
but you can watch their behavior.

00:47:00.456 --> 00:47:04.080
And they can suggest ideas
if they're of customer.

00:47:04.080 --> 00:47:07.462
And sometimes you have those right on
the leading edge customers that are always

00:47:07.462 --> 00:47:08.834
thinking about the next thing.

00:47:08.834 --> 00:47:14.450
And that's a really good
person to add to a brainstorm.

00:47:14.450 --> 00:47:17.216
But just like with a musical group,
I mean if people can't get along.

00:47:17.216 --> 00:47:22.478
If people can't listen to each other,
if they can't exchange ideas and

00:47:22.478 --> 00:47:26.272
state Stay on the then it won't work, and

00:47:26.272 --> 00:47:29.827
sometimes you mix a group and
it just falls flat.

00:47:29.827 --> 00:47:34.935
&gt;&gt; Yeah, so one request that we had
is if you can cure, an example,

00:47:34.935 --> 00:47:39.110
maybe that would be
the last custom public.

00:47:39.110 --> 00:47:43.730
If you can share an example of either,
as an outcome of a workshop or

00:47:43.730 --> 00:47:45.637
a class here at the D school.

00:47:45.637 --> 00:47:50.549
Or of the work you've done with companies
outside of Stanford where this design

00:47:50.549 --> 00:47:54.438
thinking process has created
significant business results or

00:47:54.438 --> 00:47:57.461
some other outcomes that
can give us an example?

00:47:57.461 --> 00:48:00.800
&gt;&gt; Yeah, I'll give you one,
I know it's not directly related

00:48:00.800 --> 00:48:04.610
to this one in my class, but it was in
a class called Launch Pad in the d.

00:48:04.610 --> 00:48:09.130
school, where people get together,
they form little entrepreneurial teams and

00:48:09.130 --> 00:48:10.480
they try to start a company.

00:48:10.480 --> 00:48:13.440
And the absurd thing is with our
quarters are ten weeks long, so

00:48:13.440 --> 00:48:18.830
you have to have a company with revenue in
ten weeks, which is a crazy, crazy idea.

00:48:18.830 --> 00:48:25.120
So there were two young programmers
from the CS department, who had an idea,

00:48:25.120 --> 00:48:30.370
just a very early version of
a news aggregator, like how

00:48:30.370 --> 00:48:34.200
could their generation, the millennials,
don't read the New York Times, and stuff.

00:48:34.200 --> 00:48:36.020
They just want to see snippets of news.

00:48:36.020 --> 00:48:39.130
And nobody had really
done that very well yet.

00:48:39.130 --> 00:48:42.540
This was back, I think,
three years, four years ago.

00:48:42.540 --> 00:48:46.024
And they came to Launch Pad and they
had no design thinking training at all.

00:48:46.024 --> 00:48:50.680
And but this is had an idea of how can
we get millennials to read more news?

00:48:50.680 --> 00:48:56.330
So we forced them to go out to
coffee shops all over Palo Alto,

00:48:56.330 --> 00:49:00.660
because there's lots of people in coffee
shops writing stuff, and working.

00:49:00.660 --> 00:49:06.390
And one of the programmers was painfully
shy and wouldn't get out of the car,

00:49:06.390 --> 00:49:08.910
and for the first sort of set
of ethnographic interviews.

00:49:08.910 --> 00:49:12.230
because he just didn't like,
just uncomfortable and talk to people.

00:49:12.230 --> 00:49:14.230
But they eventually broke
through that barrier and

00:49:14.230 --> 00:49:16.120
they started prototyping things.

00:49:16.120 --> 00:49:21.090
And they had some ideas about how maybe
to visually present little new snippets.

00:49:21.090 --> 00:49:24.590
And then maybe to visually
organize those new snippets.

00:49:24.590 --> 00:49:28.330
They built probably five or
six prototypes in the first three or

00:49:28.330 --> 00:49:31.760
four weeks of the visual design
of this piece of software.

00:49:31.760 --> 00:49:35.240
And then they would go over to the Coupa
Cafe in Palo Alto and just sit there.

00:49:35.240 --> 00:49:37.362
And eventually someone will walk over and
say, what are you guys doing?

00:49:37.362 --> 00:49:39.580
And they'd say,
we're working on this news thing.

00:49:39.580 --> 00:49:42.650
You want to try it?
And they would get people to try it.

00:49:42.650 --> 00:49:47.170
Week six of the quarter,
they launched their company.

00:49:47.170 --> 00:49:48.990
Week eight of the quarter,

00:49:48.990 --> 00:49:52.680
they already had first 10,000
users paying money to use this.

00:49:52.680 --> 00:49:56.270
By week ten,
they were featured in a key note speech.

00:49:56.270 --> 00:49:58.700
This was back when Steve Jobs
was still alive, so

00:49:58.700 --> 00:50:01.410
must have been longer than four years.

00:50:01.410 --> 00:50:04.170
And Steve talked about
this new news aggregator.

00:50:04.170 --> 00:50:05.980
It was called Pace.

00:50:07.150 --> 00:50:09.389
It was eventually launched
as a full company.

00:50:09.389 --> 00:50:13.120
They were the number one news
aggregator software on the web.

00:50:13.120 --> 00:50:16.313
This was before Flipbook and
some other things came along.

00:50:16.313 --> 00:50:19.113
And they were purchased
a couple of years ago for

00:50:19.113 --> 00:50:24.091
$90 million there's about $2 million
invested in the company, by LinkedIn.

00:50:24.091 --> 00:50:28.978
So the LinkedIn news feed that you see
is in fact the software that these guys

00:50:28.978 --> 00:50:29.555
wrote.

00:50:29.555 --> 00:50:33.090
They've never designed a product before,
they've never done

00:50:33.090 --> 00:50:37.640
ethnographic research or empathy before,
they were terrible at brain storming.

00:50:37.640 --> 00:50:40.084
Two CS kids,
they were very linear in their thinking.

00:50:40.084 --> 00:50:43.090
They're terrible at
brainstorming when they started.

00:50:43.090 --> 00:50:47.844
But by learning to kind of use this
process to unleash their own creativity,

00:50:47.844 --> 00:50:51.945
they're very creative,
very hard working, super sharp guys.

00:50:51.945 --> 00:50:55.263
They created a product that
kind of set the pace for

00:50:55.263 --> 00:50:59.213
our news aggregation and
really understood how millennials

00:50:59.213 --> 00:51:03.649
adopt to small clips of news that
you can go deeper on and deeper on.

00:51:04.660 --> 00:51:07.250
Came up with a really
lovely user interface.

00:51:07.250 --> 00:51:08.490
It was easy to use.

00:51:08.490 --> 00:51:12.670
And eventually it became
the basis of LinkedIn's news feed.

00:51:12.670 --> 00:51:15.820
I've got half a dozen
other examples of things.

00:51:15.820 --> 00:51:18.360
&gt;&gt; One's fine.
&gt;&gt; That's one of many, but what I like

00:51:18.360 --> 00:51:22.470
about it is it started with people
who had no design experience at all.

00:51:22.470 --> 00:51:25.020
And then ended up,
just through the process of iteration and

00:51:25.020 --> 00:51:29.090
paying attention to customers and
brainstorming a lot, they came up

00:51:29.090 --> 00:51:33.370
with something that turned out be one of
the leading products in their domain.

00:51:35.040 --> 00:51:36.330
&gt;&gt; Well, thank you so much Bill.

00:51:36.330 --> 00:51:37.740
We have many, many more questions but

00:51:37.740 --> 00:51:40.060
of course we won't have
time to answer all of them.

00:51:40.060 --> 00:51:43.564
Hopefully some of you will be able to
meet us here on campus in June and

00:51:43.564 --> 00:51:47.567
address these questions and many others
over the course of these three days.

00:51:47.567 --> 00:51:51.816
And we hope you enjoy the rest of your
day, thank you very much for joining us.

